h264_wiiu: fix the frame buffer overflow, decode every picture, return them in order

- The decoder writes NV12 with rows padded to 256 bytes and the height padded to 16 lines, but
  the frame buffer was allocated as width x height x 1.5: most sizes other than 1280x720 (852x480,
  640x360, 1920x1080...) overflowed it on every picture and corrupted the heap. The buffer now
  holds the largest picture the decoder accepts, and the chroma plane is read after the padded
  luma plane.
- The bitstream is the packet (data/size), not the whole underlying buffer, copied to a
  1024-byte-aligned buffer as Moonlight does.
- Any status with no bits above 0xff is success (0xE4 is a complete picture).
- Pictures nothing refers to (most B-frames) were always dropped, halving the frame rate of most
  streams. They are decoded now, and only left out when the caller sets skip_frame.
- With OUTPUT_PER_FRAME the decoder returns pictures in decoding order: they are reordered by
  timestamp, and the size, pitch and crop the output callback reports are used.
- Stream size changes (such as ads in a live stream) set the new dimensions.
- If the decoder rejects unreferenced pictures, they are left out from then on. The frame
  buffer is only read without a callback once the decoder has never called back in 8 pictures
  (a decoder that reorders has nothing to show for the first few).
- Warnings explain what the decoder did: its first picture, error statuses, no pictures.

